<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Erciyes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Argaeus, Erciyes Dağı</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Erciyes, the ancient Mount Argaeus, is a dormant volcano south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and at 3,917 metres the highest mountain in the region. The kings of Cappadocia ruled from Mazaca at its foot, and the mountain appeared on the city's coins. Its eruptions helped form the volcanic landscape of Cappadocia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-erciyes.jpg" alt="Mount Erciyes" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erciyes_(Argaeus),_Turkey_(31870998063)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Erciyes is a large stratovolcano in Kayseri Province, surrounded by many monogenetic vents and lava domes and one maar. Most of its bulk is formed by lava flows of andesitic and dacitic composition. It is the most voluminous volcano of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and rises about 900 metres above the Sultansazlığı basin. Its height is given as 3,864, 3,918 or 3,917 metres. Kayseri lies 15 to 25 kilometres to the north, and some lava domes lie within the city limits.</p><p>The volcano is part of the Central Anatolian Volcanic Province, which formed where the Anatolian block was squeezed between the Eurasian, African and Arabian plates. Volcanism there began in the Miocene. A volcano named Koç Dağ formed first, then large explosive eruptions made a caldera, and in the Pleistocene Erciyes itself grew inside it. Part of the summit later collapsed towards the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Erciyes is the Turkish adoption of the Greek Argaios, meaning bright or white. It was spelled Erciyas until vowel harmony changed it in the 1940s to 1960s. Assyrian sources name a mountain and god Aškašipa, and the Bronze Age Mount Harhara of Anatolian inscriptions, which may be Erciyes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> left rock carvings and an artificial tunnel on the mountain.</p><p>The geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> said the mountain was forested in antiquity and mentioned the view from its summit, from which climbers were said to see both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Strabo and Claudian report volcanic activity, and Roman coins found in Cappadocia show the mountain smoking, but these may refer to gas from swamps. The last eruptions occurred in the early Holocene. In the eleventh century Lazaros of Mount Galesios is said to have climbed it in midwinter singing psalms. A story of Cis Hatun tells that her wedding dress, left on the mountain, forms its snow cover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The volcano is considered dormant. One regular glacier remains and is receding, and the volcano was glaciated in the Pleistocene. Its eruption 2.8 million years ago produced the Valibaba Tepe ignimbrite, the last of the Cappadocian ignimbrites. The Erciyes Ski Resort lies in the Üçker valley, and the wetlands of the Sultansazlığı basin are protected under the Ramsar Convention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Erciyes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His adopted son Ariarathes II regained the land c. 301 BCE, and his heirs took the title king, ruling the plateau from Mazaca beneath <b>Mount Argaeus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Kingdom of Cappadocia</a></li></ul></section>
